@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+The original storefront now includes account settings at `/#account` and `/account`. It supports persisted profile edits, passkey nicknames, deletion, and verified WebAuthn registration with platform authenticators or security keys.
+
+## Run
+
+Requires Node.js 22 or later.
+
+```sh
+npm install
+npm start
+```
+
+Open `http://localhost:11222`. With no `ACCOUNT_PASSWORD` configured, development mode opens a local demo account automatically. It starts with no passkeys. After signing out, the demo password is `demo`. This mode is for local development only.
+
+Set `ACCOUNT_PASSWORD` to disable automatic demo sign-in. The application stores one account and its registered credentials in `data/account.json`; use `DATA_FILE` to change the location. Sessions are held in memory and expire after eight hours or on a server restart. The JSON store uses atomic replacement and supports a single server process. Credential operations enforce the authenticated account's ownership and omit public key material from list responses.
+
+`APP_ORIGIN` must be the exact browser origin, including its port; its hostname supplies the WebAuthn RP ID. It defaults to `http://localhost:11222` (or the configured `PORT`). Use HTTPS outside localhost. Requests that mutate data require a matching Origin header.
+
+## Production
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+NODE_ENV=production APP_ORIGIN=https://coffee.example ACCOUNT_PASSWORD=your-secret npm start
+```
+
+Production refuses to start without an explicit origin and password and serves the compiled frontend. For a multi-account deployment, connect the session and storage layer to your existing identity system and database. This implementation manages passkeys; it does not add passkey sign-in or authenticator-app enrollment. `lastUsedAt` is a nullable persisted epoch timestamp; a future verified sign-in flow should update it on successful authentication.
+
+## Verification
+
+```sh
+npm test
+npx playwright install chromium
+npm run test:browser
+npm run build
+```
+
+Browser tests cover initial synchronization, provider metadata and dates, empty lists, nickname edits, profile synchronization, deletion through the last credential, unavailable APIs, request failures, signal failures, and a complete verified USB security-key registration using Chromium's virtual authenticator. The API test covers authentication, ownership, validation, persistence, origin checks, and registration challenge handling.
+
+`src/aaguids.json` is a local snapshot of the [community AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids), retrieved on 2026-10-02. All 83 entries retain their provider names and light-theme icons; unused dark-theme icons are omitted. Unknown and zero AAGUIDs use a generic fallback. Provider metadata is display-only and is never used to authorize a credential.
+
+Signal API calls are feature-detected. Successful deletion refreshes the full server list before signaling it; failed fetches never signal an invented empty list. Profile saves signal the returned account details. Nickname changes do not signal profile details. The installed `webauthn-polyfills` package supplies capability detection and WebAuthn JSON conversion for older browsers, while Signal API support remains optional.
